The P0-A spike ran all three D14 cases against a live node with enforce: true.
A frozen address cannot deposit and cannot be named as a direct EVM recipient —
both refused at submission. But a pool CAN pay a frozen address: relay()
succeeded and delivered 3.96 KASH, with the freeze verified live at that moment
(the address could not send its own transaction, and was paid anyway).

That is worse than the leak D14 otherwise accepts. D14's case needs a fresh,
unlisted address the authority never had a claim on; this one needs none. The
association set, not the ante decorator, is the control that covers it — which is
the concrete reason the set's publisher is a key of the same class as the freeze
authority.

Also records an operational finding from the same run: an emergency freeze is a
holding action, not an enforcement mechanism. It expired mid-test while a proof
was generating. With a 24h mainnet timelock the window is wider but the shape is
identical, so any runbook built on emergency freeze needs an explicit
ratification step.

- ENGINEERING.md §10.1: observed results table and the operational note
- ENGINEERING.md §11 D14: marked confirmed by observation
- STATUS.md §2: P0-A completion row, with what it does and does not claim

