+**Observed 2026-09-21, not merely analysed.** The P0-A spike ran all three cases
+against a live node with `enforce: true`:
+
+| Case | Result |
+|---|---|
+| Frozen address deposits | **refused** at submission — `address is frozen` |
+| Frozen address named as a direct EVM recipient | **refused** — the decorator reads `to` |
+| Pool pays a frozen address via `relay()` | **succeeded** — 3.96 KASH delivered |
+
+In the third case the freeze was verified live at the moment of payment: the
+address could not send its own transaction, and was paid anyway. The recipient sat
+inside ABI-encoded calldata, so the decorator saw only the pool contract as `to`.
+
+That is worse than the leak D14 otherwise accepts. D14's case needs a *fresh,
+unlisted* address the authority never had a claim on; this one needs none — an
+address already on the block list can be paid out of a pool by anyone willing to
+submit the transaction. **The association set, not the ante decorator, is the
+control that covers it**, which is why the set's publisher is a key of the same
+class as the freeze authority (§10).
+
 The residual leak, if no such layer exists or the set is not curated: shield
 before a freeze, unshield to a fresh address after, bounded to funds already
 shielded at freeze time.
 
+One operational note from the same run: an **emergency freeze is a holding action,
+not an enforcement mechanism.** It expires after one timelock unless ratified, and
+on the dev chain it lapsed mid-test while a proof was being generated. With a 24 h
+mainnet timelock the window is wider but the shape is identical — any runbook built
+on emergency freeze needs an explicit ratification step, or the freeze quietly
+expires.
